    body: `
<p>English needs a helper word to say no or to ask a question: "I <i>don't</i> have", "<i>Do</i> you have?". Czech has no helper word. You change the verb itself, or just your voice.</p>
<h3>ne- goes on the front of the verb</h3>
<p>English says "I do not have". Czech puts <b>ne</b> on the front of the verb and writes it as one word: <b>mám</b> (I have) becomes <b>nemám</b> (I don't have). The ending stays the same, so the person is still in the ending.</p>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Yes</th><th>No</th><th>English</th></tr><tr><td class="k">mám</td><td class="k">nemám</td><td>I don't have</td></tr><tr><td class="k">máte</td><td class="k">nemáte</td><td>you don't have</td></tr><tr><td class="k">mluvím</td><td class="k">nemluvím</td><td>I don't speak</td></tr><tr><td class="k">studuju</td><td class="k">nestuduju</td><td>I don't study</td></tr></table></div>
{{ex:Nemám čas.|NEH-maahm chahs|I don't have time.}}
{{ex:Nemluvím česky.|NEHM-loo-veem CHEHS-kih|I don't speak Czech.}}
<h3>být: one irregular form</h3>
<p><b>jsem</b> becomes <b>nejsem</b>, <b>jste</b> becomes <b>nejste</b>, <b>jsme</b> becomes <b>nejsme</b> and <b>jsou</b> becomes <b>nejsou</b>. The odd one is <b>je</b> (is): its "not" form is <b>není</b>.</p>
{{ex:Nejsem student.|NEHY-sehm STOO-dehnt|I'm not a student.}}
{{ex:To není káva.|toh NEH-nyee KAAH-vah|That isn't coffee.}}
<h3>The beat moves to ne-</h3>
<p>Czech puts the beat on the first syllable, and <b>ne</b> is now the first syllable. So you say <b>NEH</b>-maahm, not neh-MAAHM. Do not hold the verb back as if it were a separate word.</p>
<h3>Yes/no questions</h3>
<p>English moves words around and adds "do". Czech keeps the statement exactly as it is and lets the voice rise at the end. In writing, only the question mark changes.</p>
{{ex:Máte čas?|MAAH-teh chahs|Do you have time?}}
{{ex:Mluvíte anglicky?|MLOO-vee-teh AHN-glihts-kih|Do you speak English?}}
<p>Yes is <b>ano</b> and no is <b>ne</b>. A short answer usually repeats the verb, with or without <b>ne-</b>.</p>
{{ex:Ne, nemám.|neh NEH-maahm|No, I don't.}}
<h3>Two negatives are normal</h3>
<p>English says "I don't have anything" or "I have nothing", never both. Czech uses both: <b>nic</b> (nothing) and <b>ne-</b> on the verb.</p>
{{ex:Nemám nic.|NEH-maahm nyihts|I don't have anything.}}
<p>A friend may ask you <b>Máš čas?</b> or <b>Jsi unavený?</b>. Those are the ty forms. Recognize them, and keep using <b>máte</b> and <b>jste</b> yourself.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> writing "not" as a separate word, as in English. <b>Ne mám čas.</b> → <b>Nemám čas.</b> And there is no word for "do": <b>Děláte mít čas?</b> → <b>Máte čas?</b></div>
`,
