    title: "The object form",
    blurb: "Order food and say what you have: put feminine nouns and masculine persons into the object form.",
    deck: "Food and drink",
    body: `
<p>In English, "coffee" is the same word in "Coffee is good" and "I'll have coffee". In Czech, some nouns change when they are the thing you have, order, like or look for. This course calls it the <b>object form</b>. A textbook calls it the accusative.</p>
<h3>Feminine nouns in -a: -a becomes -u</h3>
<p>This is the change you will make most often. <b>káva</b> becomes <b>kávu</b>, <b>polévka</b> becomes <b>polévku</b>, <b>voda</b> becomes <b>vodu</b>, <b>ryba</b> becomes <b>rybu</b>.</p>
{{ex:Dám si kávu.|daahm sih KAAH-voo|I'll have a coffee.}}
{{ex:Mám rád polévku.|maahm raaht POH-lehhf-koo|I like soup.}}
{{ex:Hledám Evu.|HLEH-daahm EH-voo|I'm looking for Eva.}}
<h3>Masculine: a person changes, a thing does not</h3>
<p>Masculine nouns split in two. A masculine person (a textbook says "animate") adds <b>-a</b>: <b>bratr</b> becomes <b>bratra</b>, <b>Petr</b> becomes <b>Petra</b>. A masculine thing usually stays the same: <b>čaj</b> stays <b>čaj</b>, <b>chléb</b> stays <b>chléb</b>.</p>
{{ex:Mám bratra.|maahm BRAH-trah|I have a brother.}}
{{ex:Hledám Petra.|HLEH-daahm PEH-trah|I'm looking for Petr.}}
{{ex:Dám si čaj.|daahm sih chahy|I'll have tea.}}
<h3>Neuter: no change</h3>
<p><b>pivo</b> stays <b>pivo</b>, <b>kuře</b> (chicken) stays <b>kuře</b>.</p>
{{ex:Dám si pivo.|daahm sih PIH-voh|I'll have a beer.}}
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th></th><th>Subject form</th><th>Object form</th></tr><tr><td>feminine in -a</td><td class="k">káva</td><td class="k">kávu</td></tr><tr><td>masculine person</td><td class="k">bratr</td><td class="k">bratra</td></tr><tr><td>masculine thing</td><td class="k">čaj</td><td class="k">čaj</td></tr><tr><td>neuter</td><td class="k">pivo</td><td class="k">pivo</td></tr></table></div>
<h3>Where you need it</h3>
<p>After <b>mám</b> (I have), <b>mám rád</b> (I like), <b>hledám</b> (I'm looking for) and <b>dám si</b> (I'll have). <b>Dám si</b> is how you order: literally "I'll give myself". The little word <b>si</b> is unstressed and sits second in the sentence.</p>
<p>You will meet more patterns later. For now, these four rows cover most of what you will order or talk about. A question keeps the object form too: <b>Máte kávu?</b>, "Do you have coffee?". The subject form stays where it was: <b>Káva je dobrá</b>. The coffee is doing the "being", so it is the subject, not the object.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> leaving the dictionary form in the object slot. <b>Dám si káva.</b> → <b>Dám si kávu.</b> And for a person: <b>Mám bratr.</b> → <b>Mám bratra.</b></div>
`,
